course: books
lesson: 13
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter VII, part 1: A Mad Tea-Party (no room, riddles and Time)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Alice reaches the March Hare's house and finds a tea-table under a tree. The March Hare and the Hatter are squashed into one corner, leaning their elbows on a sleeping Dormouse as if it were a cushion. «No room! No room!» they shout, though the table is huge, so Alice sits down in a big arm-chair anyway, cross.
explain: Everything they say is rude or upside down. The March Hare offers her wine that is not there, the Hatter says her hair needs cutting, then he asks a riddle: «Why is a raven like a writing-desk?» Alice says she can guess it, and they tease her for mixing up «saying what you mean» and «meaning what you say».
explain: The Hatter's watch shows the day of the month, not the hour, and it is two days wrong, because the March Hare put butter in it, «the best butter». The March Hare dips it in his tea and gloomily repeats that it was the best butter.
explain: When Alice asks the answer to the riddle, the Hatter says he has not the slightest idea. Alice grumbles that they are wasting time, and the Hatter says Time is a person, «him», who will move the clock for you if you are friends.
explain: But the Hatter quarrelled with Time last March, at the Queen of Hearts' concert, while he was singing «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat! How I wonder what you're at!» Alice feels puzzled all through: the words are English, but the sense keeps slipping away.
fact: The Cheshire Cat told Alice in chapter VI that a Hatter and a March Hare live nearby and that «they're both mad»; she chose the March Hare.
fact: At the table sit the March Hare, the Hatter and the Dormouse, who is fast asleep and used as a cushion.
fact: They cry «No room! No room!»; Alice answers «There's plenty of room!» and sits in a large arm-chair at one end.
fact: The March Hare offers wine, but there is only tea; Alice says it «wasn't very civil» (civil = polite) to offer it, and he says it wasn't civil of her to sit down without being invited.
fact: «Your hair wants cutting» means your hair needs cutting; Alice tells the Hatter not to make personal remarks, rude comments about someone's looks.
fact: The riddle is «Why is a raven like a writing-desk?»; later the Hatter says «I haven't the slightest idea» and the March Hare says «Nor I».
fact: The March Hare tells Alice «Then you should say what you mean»; the Hatter shows that turning words round changes the sense: «I see what I eat» is not the same as «I eat what I see».
fact: For the Dormouse, «I breathe when I sleep» and «I sleep when I breathe» really are the same, because he is nearly always asleep.
fact: Alice says the date is «The fourth»; the Hatter sighs «Two days wrong!» and blames the March Hare for putting butter in the watch with the bread-knife, so crumbs got in.
fact: The Hatter's watch tells the day of the month, not the hour; he says Alice's watch does not tell the year either; she says that is because the year «stays the same year for such a long time together», and he answers «Which is just the case with mine», as if for him the hour stayed the same just as long; Alice feels «dreadfully puzzled».
fact: «The Dormouse is asleep again,» says the Hatter, and he pours a little hot tea upon its nose.
fact: The Hatter talks of Time as a person: «It's him.» If you kept on good terms with him, he says, Time would jump the clock from nine o'clock lessons to half-past one, dinner-time (dinner = the midday meal in old English homes); when Alice says she wouldn't be hungry yet, he answers «you could keep it to half-past one as long as you liked».
fact: «Beat time» in music means tapping the beat; the Hatter pretends it means hitting Time, who «won't stand beating».
fact: «I dare say» means I suppose; «that accounts for it» means that explains it; «in a twinkling» means in a flash.
fact: «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat! How I wonder what you're at!» (it goes on «Up above the world you fly, Like a tea-tray in the sky») is a silly copy of the children's song «Twinkle, twinkle, little star», just as Alice's poems in chapters II and V came out wrong.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «innit» (London) = isn't it; «no cap» = no lie, honestly; «mardy» (North and Midlands England) = sulky; «yinz» (Pittsburgh, USA) = you lot; «canny» (North East England) = quite, or nice.
exercise: Whose house is the tea-table in front of? | The March Hare's | The Hatter's own shop | The Duchess's, by the kitchen door | The White Rabbit's, with W. RABBIT on the door
exercise: Who sits between the March Hare and the Hatter? | The Dormouse, fast asleep | The Cheshire Cat, grinning widely | Alice, in a small chair | Nobody at all
exercise: What do the March Hare and the Hatter do with the Dormouse? | Rest their elbows on him like a cushion | Send him off to fetch more milk and sugar from the house | Use him to keep their teapot hot | Ask him riddles to keep him awake
exercise: What do they cry out when they see Alice coming? | «No room! No room!» | «Off with her head!» | «Come and have tea, dear!» | «Mary Ann, fetch my gloves!»
exercise: Where does Alice sit down? | In a large arm-chair at one end of the table | On the Dormouse | On a three-legged stool in the middle, next to the teapot | On the grass under the tree
exercise: The March Hare offers Alice something to drink. What is the trick? | He offers wine, but there is only tea on the table | He offers tea, but the pot is quite empty inside | He offers milk, but the jug turns out to be full of sticky treacle | He offers lemonade, but it is really pepper water
exercise: «Then it wasn't very civil of you to offer it,» says Alice. What does civil mean here? | Polite | Cheap | Clever | Quiet
exercise: What does the March Hare say back about civil manners? | It wasn't civil of her to sit down without being invited | It was very civil, as wine is a good drink | Civil people never drink tea at six o'clock | She should go and ask the Queen of Hearts what civil really means
exercise: «Your hair wants cutting,» says the Hatter. What does he mean in today's English? | Your hair needs a cut | Your hair is asking me for scissors | Your hair is far too short already | I would like to sell you a hat
exercise: What does Alice tell the Hatter after his remark about her hair? | That he should not make personal remarks, as it is very rude | That she will cut it herself with her sister's scissors as soon as she gets home | That her sister cut it only the week before | That his own hat needs a good brush, which is true
exercise: What riddle does the Hatter ask? | «Why is a raven like a writing-desk?» | «Why is a mouse like a teapot?» | «What has a head but never thinks?» | «Why does the Dormouse never wake?»
exercise: Alice says «I believe I can guess that». What does the March Hare say she should do? | Say what she means | Keep her temper | Guess it in French | Take more tea first
exercise: What day of the month does Alice say it is? | The fourth | The fourteenth | The sixth | The first
exercise: The Hatter says the watch is «two days wrong». What does he blame? | Butter that the March Hare put in the works, with crumbs | Tea that the Dormouse spilt into it this morning | The Queen, who stamped on it hard at the great concert last March | Alice, who looked at it over his shoulder
exercise: What does the March Hare keep saying about the butter? | «It was the best butter» | «It was only a little butter» | «It was the Hatter's butter» | «Butter is good for watches»
exercise: What does the March Hare do with the watch? | Dips it into his cup of tea | Puts it into the teapot with the Dormouse | Gives it to Alice to keep | Throws it into the treacle
exercise: What does the Hatter do to wake the Dormouse? | Pours a little hot tea upon its nose | Pinches its tail very hard | Sings «Twinkle, twinkle» into its ear | Shouts «The Queen! The Queen!»
exercise: What is the answer to the raven riddle, according to the Hatter? | He has not the slightest idea | The March Hare knows, but won't tell | Both are black and full of feathers | Both have legs and live indoors
exercise: «If you knew Time as well as I do...» How does the Hatter talk about Time? | As a person, «him», who can be a friend or an enemy | As a thing that can be bought in a shop for a shilling | As a kind of watch that only works with butter | As the name of the Queen's favourite soldier
exercise: According to the Hatter, what could Time do for a friend at nine o'clock lessons? | Move the clock round to half-past one, dinner-time | Make lessons last all day long | Stop the clock at nine o'clock for ever, so lessons never end | Turn the lesson-books into cake
exercise: Why, the Hatter says, did Time stop obeying him? | They quarrelled at the Queen of Hearts' concert last March | The Hatter forgot to wind his watch for a week | Time was angry that the Hatter beat him with a tea spoon at breakfast | Time wanted a seat at the tea-table and got none
exercise: What song did the Hatter have to sing at the concert? | «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat!» | «How doth the little crocodile» | «You are old, Father William» | «Twinkle, twinkle, little star!»
exercise: In the Hatter's song, the bat flies «like a» what? | A tea-tray in the sky | A diamond in the sky | A kite in the sky | A cat in the sky
exercise: «That accounts for it,» says the Hatter. What does «accounts for» mean? | Explains | Counts up | Pays for | Writes down
exercise: How does Alice feel about the Hatter's remark on watches and years? | Dreadfully puzzled: it seemed to have no meaning, yet it was certainly English | Very pleased, because she guessed what he meant at once and told the March Hare so | Frightened, because the Hatter shouted it in her face | Bored, so she fell asleep beside the Dormouse
test: Mo says: «she sat down cos they invited her, innit.» (innit = isn't it) Put it right. | Nobody invited her: they shouted «No room!» and she sat anyway | Right, the March Hare showed her to the arm-chair politely | She was sent there by the Queen of Hearts with a letter | The Dormouse woke up and kindly gave her his own place
test: «Hatter knew the raven answer, he just wouldn't tell, no cap.» (no cap = honestly) Is that true? | No: he said «I haven't the slightest idea» | Yes: he whispered it to the March Hare behind his hand | Yes: it was «both are black» | No: he said Alice knew it
test: The table «was a large one», but the three were «crowded together at one corner». What does this tell us? | «No room!» was not true; there was plenty of room | The rest of the table was broken | The Queen had booked the other seats | The table was too hot to sit at
test: Why is «I breathe when I sleep» the same as «I sleep when I breathe» for the Dormouse? | He is asleep nearly all the time, so whenever he breathes he is sleeping | He only ever breathes at night, when the March Hare and the Hatter are asleep too | He holds his breath all day and only breathes in bed at night | Dormice do not breathe, so both sentences are equally false
test: What would happen at a school if a child were friends with Time, as the Hatter explains it? | Time could whizz the clock from lessons to dinner | Lessons would last for ever | The teacher would turn into a hare | Dinner would be served at breakfast-time
test: Alice says she would not be hungry at half-past one if Time jumped forward. What does the Hatter answer? | You could keep it at half-past one as long as you liked | You would be hungry at once, because Time decides that too | You must eat whatever happens, or the Queen will be angry | Time would bring the dinner as well as the clock
test: Leah from Pittsburgh: «yinz all know the Hatter's watch tells the time, like o'clock.» (yinz = you lot) Put it right. | It tells the day of the month, not what o'clock it is | It tells the year, the month, the hour and the minute all together | It tells nothing at all, because it has no hands | It only tells the time when it is dipped in tea
test: Alice's watch does not tell the year «because it stays the same year for such a long time». Why does the Hatter say «Which is just the case with mine»? | His watch shows no hours, as if the hour were always the same | His watch is broken and shows nothing at all | His watch belongs to the Queen and tells her years | His watch shows the year, which is always the same one
test: Tom, a bit mardy (sulky): «March Hare put cheese in the watch, that's why it's wrong.» Put it right. | It was butter, «the best butter», and crumbs got in with it | It was strawberry jam that the Dormouse spread all over the works | It was tea, which the Hatter poured in to clean it | It was treacle, from the bottom of a treacle-well
test: Which is TRUE about the tea-party so far? | The Hatter asks a riddle that nobody can answer | Alice is invited and given wine | The Dormouse is wide awake and cheerful | The Hatter is polite and quiet all the time
test: The Hatter says Time «won't stand beating». What joke does he make? | «Beat time» means tap the beat in music, but he pretends it means hit Time | «Beating» means winning a race, and Time hates to lose | Time is a big drum, and drums don't like being beaten too often by hatters | Beating eggs takes too long, so Time ran off
test: Looking back: which creature first told Alice where the Hatter and the March Hare live? | The Cheshire Cat, waving one paw and then the other | The Caterpillar, sitting on top of the mushroom with folded arms | The Pigeon in the tree-top | The Frog-Footman by the door
test: Looking back: in chapter VI Alice went to the March Hare's thinking «as this is May» he would be what? | Not quite so mad as he was in March | Asleep, like the Dormouse | Away at the Queen's croquet | Busy painting roses red
test: Looking back: who else in the book has a watch, and how is it different? | The White Rabbit, who took his from his waistcoat-pocket to check if he was late | The Duchess, who wore hers on a long chain round the baby's neck in the kitchen | The Caterpillar, who smoked beside a clock on the mushroom | The Mouse, whose watch told the history of William the Conqueror
test: Looking back: «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat» comes out wrong, like poems earlier in the book. Which one did Alice get wrong for the Caterpillar? | «You are old, Father William» | «Twinkle, twinkle, little star» | «Speak roughly to your little boy» | «Will you walk a little faster?»
test: Looking back: what did Alice do to grow to about two feet high before going to the March Hare's house? | Nibbled the left-hand bit of the mushroom | Drank from a bottle marked DRINK ME | Ate a cake with EAT ME in currants | Picked up the White Rabbit's fan
test: Looking back: falling down the rabbit-hole, Alice wondered «Do cats eat bats?» Why is the «little bat» song a nice echo of that? | Bats come up in Alice's head again, mixed into a nonsense song | Dinah the cat sings the song at the Queen's concert | The Hatter is really a bat in disguise | Alice wrote the song while she was falling
test: ★★★ «I see what I eat» and «I eat what I see». Which pair below changes its meaning in the same way when turned round? | «I like what I get» and «I get what I like» | «I sit and I eat» and «I eat and I sit» | «Tea and bread» and «bread and tea» | «Hare and Hatter» and «Hatter and Hare»
test: ★★★ Alice says it is «The fourth», and she is right; the watch is «two days wrong». Which days could the watch be showing? | The second or the sixth | Only the fourth | The fourteenth or the fortieth | Six o'clock or two o'clock
test: ★★★ Careful: who was singing when the Hatter quarrelled with Time, and who quarrelled? | The Hatter sang, and the Hatter and Time quarrelled | The Queen sang, and the Queen and Time quarrelled | The March Hare sang, and the Hatter quarrelled with him | The Dormouse sang, and Time quarrelled with the Queen
exam: Identify the reason adduced by the Hatter for the inaccuracy of his watch to the extent of two days. | The introduction of butter into its works, accompanied by crumbs | The immersion of the said watch in a cup of hot tea by the Dormouse | The intervention of the Queen of Hearts at a concert held in March | The failure of the March Hare to wind it on the fourth day of the month
exam: It is given that Alice was offered wine where none was present. Select the response which most accurately characterises her rejoinder. | That the offer was not very civil | That she preferred a lemonade instead | That wine ought to be served at six o'clock and at no other hour | That the Hare should fetch it forthwith
exam: Ascertain the answer furnished by the Hatter in respect of the riddle «Why is a raven like a writing-desk?» | That he had not the slightest idea | That both were black in colour | That the Dormouse alone was in possession of it | That ravens are frequently employed in writing letters
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the Hatter's relations with Time? | A quarrel arose at a concert of the Queen of Hearts, whereupon Time ceased to oblige him | Time is to be understood as a watch belonging to the March Hare, lubricated with butter of the best quality | Time and the Hatter remain on excellent terms | Time was beheaded at the Queen's command
